<li><a href="#industry-trends--ai-search-statistics">Industry Trends &amp; AI Search Statistics (91–100)</a></li>
</ol>
</nav>
<hr />
<h2>Local SEO Statistics</h2>
<p><strong>Local SEO is the highest-ROI marketing channel for home service businesses.</strong> The data below proves why your Google Business Profile and local search presence deserve your attention before any other tactic.</p>
<h3>Stat 1</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 46% of all Google searches have local intent.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Google / Search Engine Roundtable, 2018 (behavioral pattern confirmed through 2025)<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Nearly half of all searches on the world's largest search engine are looking for something nearby. For contractors, this means your potential customers are already searching — the question is whether they find you.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> If you are not optimizing for local search, you are invisible to nearly half of your addressable market. Start with Google Business Profile optimization before spending on paid ads.</p>
<h3>Stat 2</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 76% of people who search for something nearby visit a business within 24 hours.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Google / Ipsos, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Local search is not just research — it is immediate action. Homeowners with a burst pipe or broken AC do not bookmark your website for later. They call the first credible option they find.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Your Google Business Profile must be optimized for immediate contact. Click-to-call buttons, accurate hours, and instant response systems convert local searchers into same-day revenue.</p>
<h3>Stat 3</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 78% of local mobile searches result in an offline purchase.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Backlinko / SEO.AI, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Mobile local search has the highest purchase intent of any digital channel. A homeowner searching "emergency plumber near me" on their phone is not browsing — they are buying.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Mobile optimization is not optional. Your website must load fast, display click-to-call prominently, and eliminate form friction for mobile users.</p>
<h3>Stat 4</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> "Near me" searches have grown over 500% in recent years.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Google Trends, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The phrase "near me" has become the default search behavior for local services. Homeowners no longer type "plumber in Dallas" — they say "plumber near me" and let their device handle location.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Optimize your content and Google Business Profile for "near me" intent. Include neighborhood names, service areas, and location-specific language throughout your site.</p>
<h3>Stat 5</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 97% of consumers use the internet to find local businesses, primarily through Google Business Profile.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Multiple industry sources, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The Yellow Pages are dead. Your Google Business Profile is your new storefront, directory listing, and first impression — all in one.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Treat your GBP as a full-time marketing channel, not a one-time setup. Post updates weekly, upload photos monthly, and respond to every review.</p>
<h3>Stat 6</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Businesses with a complete Google Business Profile are 2.7x more likely to be seen as reputable.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Google, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Profile completeness is a trust signal. A business with no photos, no description, and no posts looks abandoned — even if it is thriving.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Fill out every field in your GBP. Add services, products, attributes, Q&amp;A, and 20+ photos. The 30 minutes of setup work pays dividends in perceived credibility.</p>
<h3>Stat 7</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Businesses in the Google 3-Pack receive 126% more traffic than ranks 4–10.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Backlinko, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The top three local results capture the majority of clicks, traffic, and calls. Position four might as well be page two for local intent searches.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Your local SEO goal is the 3-Pack, not just page one. Focus on review velocity, category accuracy, and proximity optimization to break into the top three.</p>
<h3>Stat 8</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Businesses with 100+ photos on their Google Business Profile receive 520% more calls and 1,065% more website clicks than businesses with fewer photos.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry aggregation data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Photos are engagement magnets on GBP. They show real work, real trucks, real teams — the visual proof homeowners need before calling.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Upload 10+ geo-tagged photos monthly. Include before/after shots, team photos, completed projects, and your branded vehicles.</p>
<h3>Stat 9</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 49.3% of all Google searches carry local intent as of 2026.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal / Google Search Trends, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Local intent is growing, not shrinking. As voice search and AI assistants become more common, location-based queries continue to rise.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Your local SEO investment should increase year over year, not stay flat. Competitors who double down on local will capture market share from those who do not.</p>
<h3>Stat 10</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> "Near me" searches grew 28.7% year-over-year in 2025.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The growth rate of "near me" searches outpaces general search growth, indicating that local intent is becoming more embedded in consumer behavior.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Create location-specific landing pages for every city and neighborhood you serve. The more granular your local content, the more "near me" searches you capture.</p>
<h3>Stat 11</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 93% of local searches display the Local Pack (map results).<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The Local Pack appears on almost every local query, pushing organic results below the fold on mobile. If you are not in the Pack, you are not visible.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Local Pack optimization is more important than traditional organic ranking for service businesses. Prioritize GBP signals over general website SEO.</p>
<h3>Stat 12</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 70% of consumers are more likely to appear in AI-generated local recommendations if their Google Business Profile is complete and active.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> GrowthPro AI, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> AI search tools like ChatGPT and Gemini pull from GBP data when recommending local businesses. An incomplete profile means AI systems cannot cite you.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> GEO (Generative Engine Optimization) starts with GBP completeness. Update your profile weekly to stay visible in AI-driven search results.</p>
<h3>Stat 13</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 67% of AI Overviews for local queries reference Google Business Profile data directly.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Digital Applied, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Google's AI Overviews are increasingly common in local search, and they pull heavily from GBP. Your profile content directly influences what AI says about you.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Write your GBP description and Q&amp;A answers as if an AI will quote them. Be specific, factual, and include your key service differentiators.</p>
<h3>Stat 14</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 64% of users contact businesses using the phone number displayed on their Google Business Profile.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The phone number on your GBP is your primary conversion path. Most local searchers never visit your website — they call directly from the listing.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Use a call tracking number on your GBP to measure ROI. Ensure someone answers 24/7, or route after-hours calls to an answering service.</p>
<h3>Stat 15</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Only 35% of small businesses have a Google Business Profile.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> SMB Marketing Report, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Two-thirds of your competitors are not even on the field. This is one of the lowest-hanging fruits in home service marketing.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> If you do not have a GBP, create one today. If you do, audit it for completeness. The competitive gap is massive and the barrier to entry is nearly zero.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Website &amp; Conversion Statistics</h2>
<p><strong>Your website is your 24/7 sales rep.</strong> These statistics reveal how homeowners interact with contractor websites and what separates high-converting sites from digital brochures.</p>
<h3>Stat 16</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 65% of all website traffic comes from mobile devices.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Multiple industry sources, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Homeowners search for contractors on their phones while standing in a flooded basement or sitting in a hot living room. Desktop-first design is a conversion killer.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Design your website mobile-first. Test every page on a phone. If a homeowner cannot tap your phone number with one thumb, you are losing jobs.</p>
<h3>Stat 17</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Mobile converts 42% lower than desktop despite representing 65% of traffic.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry conversion benchmarks, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The mobile conversion gap is widening. More traffic comes from mobile, but mobile users abandon at higher rates due to slow load times and poor UX.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Close the mobile conversion gap with click-to-call buttons, simplified forms, and one-tap appointment booking. Mobile-optimized sites can reach 2.8% conversion rates.</p>
<h3>Stat 18</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> A 1-second delay in page load time reduces conversions by up to 20% on mobile.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Google / Cloudflare, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Speed is not a technical vanity metric — it is a revenue metric. Every extra second of load time costs you one in five potential leads.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Audit your site speed with Google PageSpeed Insights. Compress images, enable caching, and use a CDN. Target under 3-second load times.</p>
<h3>Stat 19</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 53% of mobile users abandon a site that takes longer than 3 seconds to load.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Google, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The 3-second threshold is the point of no return. Homeowners in crisis mode have zero patience for slow websites.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> If your site loads in 4+ seconds, you are losing more than half your mobile traffic before they even see your offer. Speed optimization is emergency-level priority.</p>
<h3>Stat 20</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 79% of users are unlikely to return to a site after a slow experience.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> A slow website does not just lose the current visit — it loses the customer permanently. First impressions are digital and permanent.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Website speed is a retention issue, not just a conversion issue. Invest in performance optimization as a core marketing strategy.</p>
<h3>Stat 21</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> The average website conversion rate for home services is 2.8% for organic search traffic.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry benchmarks, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> This means 97.2% of your website visitors leave without contacting you. The opportunity cost of poor conversion optimization is massive.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> A/B test your headlines, CTAs, and form fields. Even a 0.5% conversion rate improvement can double your lead volume without increasing traffic.</p>
<h3>Stat 22</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Dedicated service landing pages convert at 3–4x the rate of homepage traffic.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> CRO industry data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Homeowners searching "AC repair" do not want your homepage — they want a page about AC repair with pricing, testimonials, and a booking form.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Build separate landing pages for every service you offer. Each page should have a unique headline, specific social proof, and a single clear CTA.</p>
<h3>Stat 23</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Reducing form fields from 11 to 4 can increase conversions by 120%.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> CRO case study aggregation, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Every form field you add is a friction point. Homeowners in emergency mode will abandon a long form and call your competitor instead.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Limit contact forms to name, phone, and service needed. Collect additional details during the follow-up call, not on the website.</p>
<h3>Stat 24</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Adding video to a landing page can increase conversions by 80%.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Wyzowl / Landingi, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Video builds trust faster than text. A 60-second video of your team, your process, and your completed work answers objections before they arise.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Add a short trust-building video above the fold on your highest-traffic service pages. Keep it under 90 seconds and optimize for mobile (captions required).</p>
<h3>Stat 25</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 92% of videos are watched without sound.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Mobile users scroll with their phones on silent. If your video relies on voiceover, most viewers miss the message entirely.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Always add captions to website videos. Use visual storytelling that communicates value even with the sound off.</p>
<h3>Stat 26</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Websites with trust badges (license, insurance, BBB, certifications) see 30–60% higher conversion rates.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> CoalMarch, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Homeowners are inviting strangers into their homes. Trust signals reduce perceived risk and increase willingness to call.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Display your license number, insurance verification, BBB rating, and trade certifications prominently above the fold on every page.</p>
<h3>Stat 27</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 47% of users expect a web page to load in 2 seconds or less.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Akamai / Google, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Consumer expectations for speed are higher than ever. The average contractor website loads in 4–6 seconds — double what users expect.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Set a 2-second load time target. Use lazy loading, image compression, and minimal plugins to get there.</p>
<h3>Stat 28</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Direct traffic converts at 4.4% on average — higher than any other channel.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry conversion benchmarks, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Direct traffic represents people who already know your brand. They type your URL directly because they remember you from a referral, a truck wrap, or a previous job.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Brand building drives the highest-converting traffic. Invest in truck wraps, yard signs, and community visibility to increase direct traffic.</p>
<h3>Stat 29</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Organic search traffic converts at 2.8–3.1% for service businesses.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Industry benchmarks, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Organic search converts higher than paid social (2.1%) and display ads (0.5%) because searchers have active intent.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> SEO should be your primary long-term investment. It delivers higher-intent traffic at a lower cost per lead than most paid channels.</p>
<h3>Stat 30</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 97–98% of website visitors remain anonymous and never fill out a form.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> PipelineOn, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Almost everyone who visits your site leaves without identifying themselves. Retargeting and remarketing are essential to capture this lost traffic.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Install the Meta Pixel and Google Ads remarketing tag on your site. Run retargeting campaigns to bring anonymous visitors back with specific offers.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Reviews &amp; Reputation Statistics</h2>
<p><strong>Reviews are the new currency of trust.</strong> These statistics prove that your star rating and review volume directly impact revenue, ranking, and customer acquisition.</p>
<h3>Stat 31</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 97% of consumers read online reviews for local businesses.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal Local Consumer Review Survey, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Nearly every potential customer reads reviews before calling. A business with no reviews is a business with no credibility.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Reviews are not optional — they are infrastructure. Build a systematic review generation process that triggers after every completed job.</p>
<h3>Stat 32</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 41% of consumers always read reviews before choosing a local business — up from 29% in 2025.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Review dependency is accelerating. The percentage of consumers who always check reviews grew by 41% in a single year.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Your review strategy must keep pace with rising consumer expectations. What worked in 2023 is insufficient in 2025.</p>
<h3>Stat 33</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 68% of consumers will not use a business with fewer than 4 stars.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The minimum viable star rating has risen. A 3.8-star business is effectively invisible to two-thirds of the market.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Your target is 4.5+ stars. Address negative reviews promptly, fix systemic service issues, and actively generate positive reviews to maintain a high average.</p>
<h3>Stat 34</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 31% of consumers now require a 4.5+ star rating — nearly double the 17% from 2025.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Consumer standards are rising fast. The bar for "good enough" is now 4.5 stars, not 4.0.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Do not settle for 4.0 stars. Aim for 4.7+ by delivering exceptional service and asking satisfied customers for reviews while the positive experience is fresh.</p>
<h3>Stat 35</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 74% of consumers only trust reviews from the last 3 months.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Review recency matters more than review volume. A business with 500 old reviews looks less active than one with 50 recent reviews.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Maintain a review velocity of 5–10 new reviews per month. Old reviews lose value quickly — consistent generation is more important than total count.</p>
<h3>Stat 36</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 89% of consumers expect businesses to respond to reviews.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Review response is now a baseline expectation, not a nice-to-have. Consumers judge your customer service by how you handle public feedback.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Respond to every review — positive and negative — within 24 hours. Use responses as marketing: thank reviewers, address concerns, and reinforce your brand values.</p>
<h3>Stat 37</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 80% of consumers are more likely to use a business that responds to all reviews.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Review response is a competitive differentiator. Most businesses ignore negative reviews or respond with generic templates.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Write personalized, specific responses. Mention the customer's name, reference their service, and show that real humans run your business.</p>
<h3>Stat 38</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 50% of consumers are put off by generic or templated review responses.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Copy-paste responses signal that you do not care. Consumers can spot a template from a mile away.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Train your team to write unique responses. Even a 2-sentence personalized reply outperforms a 5-sentence template.</p>
<h3>Stat 39</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 93% of consumers have made a purchase after reading reviews.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Reviews do not just influence decisions — they drive purchases. The path from review reading to revenue is direct and measurable.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Track review-driven revenue. Use call tracking and CRM attribution to measure how many jobs originate from review-influenced searches.</p>
<h3>Stat 40</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 94% of consumers are open to writing a review if asked.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The barrier to review generation is not customer willingness — it is business follow-through. Almost everyone will write a review if you simply ask.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Build an automated review request system. Send a text message with a direct Google review link within 24 hours of job completion.</p>
<h3>Stat 41</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 83% of consumers who were asked for a review actually wrote one.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2026<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> The conversion rate from ask to review is incredibly high. Most businesses fail at review generation because they never ask, not because customers refuse.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Make review requests part of your standard workflow. The technician should mention it on-site, and the office should send the link same-day.</p>
<h3>Stat 42</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> A 1-star rating improvement can increase revenue by 5–9%.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Harvard Business School research, widely cited 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Star rating directly correlates with revenue. Moving from 3.8 to 4.8 stars is not vanity — it is a 5–9% revenue increase.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Calculate the revenue impact of a 1-star improvement for your business. A $2M company gains $100,000–$180,000 annually from a higher rating.</p>
<h3>Stat 43</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Displaying reviews on your website increases conversion rates by 19.8%.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> Trusthero / industry data, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Reviews on your website act as social proof for visitors who found you through non-review channels (direct traffic, referrals, paid ads).<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Embed a review widget on your homepage and service pages. Pull in Google reviews dynamically so they stay current.</p>
<h3>Stat 44</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 46% of consumers suspect AI-written reviews, and 75% are concerned about fake reviews.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal / industry surveys, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Review authenticity is under threat. Consumers are becoming more skeptical, which makes genuine, detailed reviews even more valuable.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Encourage detailed reviews that mention specific services, technicians by name, and project details. Specificity signals authenticity.</p>
<h3>Stat 45</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 76% of consumers trust mixed reviews more than perfect 5-star ratings.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> BrightLocal, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> A perfect 5.0 looks suspicious. Consumers expect some negative feedback and judge you by how you handle it.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Do not panic over occasional negative reviews. Respond professionally, take conversations offline when appropriate, and let your overall rating tell the story.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Lead Generation Statistics</h2>
<p><strong>Lead generation is where marketing meets money.</strong> These statistics reveal the true cost of leads, the importance of speed, and which channels deliver the best ROI.</p>
<h3>Stat 46</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> The average cost per lead for home improvement businesses is $90.92.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> WordStream / LocaliQ, 2025 (analysis of 3,200+ campaigns)<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> This is the blended average across all paid channels. Your actual CPL depends on trade, market, and channel mix.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Track CPL by channel (Google Ads, LSA, Facebook, SEO, referrals). Double down on channels under $90 and audit channels over $150.</p>
<h3>Stat 47</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> HVAC cost per lead averages $92 ($115 paid / $69 organic).<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> FirstPageSage, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Organic leads cost 40% less than paid leads for HVAC. The gap between paid and organic CPL exists across all trades.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Prioritize SEO and GBP optimization to reduce dependence on paid leads. Organic leads are cheaper, higher-intent, and more sustainable.</p>
<h3>Stat 48</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> Roofing cost per lead averages $228 on Google Ads — the highest of any home service trade.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> LocaliQ / PipelineOn, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Roofing leads are expensive because the job values are high ($10,000–$50,000+). The high CPL is justified by high customer lifetime value.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Roofing companies need larger marketing budgets and better lead nurturing. A $228 lead that closes into a $20,000 roof replacement is a 87:1 ROI.</p>
<h3>Stat 49</h3>
<p><strong>Statistic:</strong> 78% of customers hire the first contractor to respond.<br />
<strong>Source:</strong> HomeAdvisor / MIT studies, 2025<br />
<strong>Context:</strong> Speed is the single biggest competitive advantage in lead response. Being good matters less than being first.<br />
<strong>Implication:</strong> Respond to every lead within 5 minutes. Use auto-responders, text alerts, and answering services to ensure no lead waits.</p>
<h3>Stat 50</h3>
